    rdf:value """Mr. Fenimore Cooper, grandson of the novelist. A splendid sleigh was waiting, and we drove to Mr. Cooper’s to see a “Holbein” (I don’t think it was one), and then to Mr. Thacker’s for lunch. In the afternoon I gave my lecture and had a “tea”, and then a very interesting dinner at the Richmonds. Mr. and Mrs. Parsons were the other guests - great friends of the President’s. She is the head of the State Education Bureau.
Here in Albany, for the first time, I have “touched America”. Their heartiness and friendliness is beyond description!
Bernhard went in the morning to Glaenzer’s, and then to lunch with La Farge at
the Chapman’s. They made out Sargent as a very brutal sort of cad, such, in fact, as you would judge him from his pictures, but we had always """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 118 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1903-04> .

